# STATUS: BUILDS AND SHIPS. `make overlay` produces
# scx-scheds-1.1.3-1.2-aarch64.pkg.tar.zst carrying scx_lavd + scx_bpfland + scx_rusty
# (aarch64 ELF), scx.service and /etc/default/scx. It is in rootfs/customize-base.sh PKGS.
#
# HW-VALIDATED on SM8650, 2026-08-27, and the A/B is unusually clean because bare 1.1.3 failed so
# fast. Bare 1.1.3 self-ejected with `RCU CPU stall detected!` every 21-35s of gameplay; patched
# 1.1.3-1.2 ran 5m05s with ZERO `runnable_at=-`, `RCU CPU stall detected`, `runnable task stall`
# or `disabled (runtime error)` lines — every disable in the journal is a deliberate
# `unregistered from user space`. Roughly 10x the previous time-to-failure, signature gone.
#
# To re-gate after any scx bump: `novadeck-scheduler lavd` plus a real gameplay leg on a title
# that runs BELOW the frame cap, then `journalctl -k -g sched_ext`. PASS is the absence of those
# four markers. The `Writing directly to p->scx.slice/dsq_vtime is deprecated` warnings DO still
# appear and are EXPECTED — see the patch block below for why that site is left unpatched.
#
# pkgrel_suffix is 2, not 1, ON PURPOSE: 1.1.3-1.1 (unpatched, stalling) was already published
# in card/ota v0.2.8, so the fixed build has to outrank it or pacman keeps what it has.
#
# scx_lavd is NOT the default CPU scheduler — it is opt-in. The default is "none" (the stock
# in-kernel EEVDF).
#
# The original reason for that default is DEAD: lavd was once REPORTED to cost in-game performance,
# and on 2026-08-09 that report was measured on SM8650 and REFUTED. An A/B/A sweep on two titles
# found lavd FASTER, decisively so once a game has headroom below the frame cap:
#
#   Darksiders II (DX11 via Proton)  stock 30.4 / 31.9 fps  ->  lavd 49.6 / 51.5 fps   (~+62%)
#   Trine EE (near the 60 cap)       stock 58.6 / 58.5 fps  ->  lavd 59.5 fps          (~+1.7%)
#
# Mechanism, reproducible across every stock capture: EEVDF leaves the cap-1024 prime core almost
# idle (1.3-3.3%) and piles onto the mid cores, while lavd loads it to 23-38% and uses all 8 CPUs.
# Also measured: --performance (core compaction OFF) is NOT better than the shipped --autopower, so
# leave the flags alone.
#
# The default stays "none" as a deliberate policy choice, not because of the refuted report: lavd is
# a non-default in-kernel scheduler swap, so it is opted into rather than imposed. A device opts in
# with `novadeck-scheduler lavd`, via the org.novadeck.Power1.CpuScheduler property, or per-game and
# globally from novadeck-control. Note a device that booted while lavd WAS the default keeps it — an
# explicit saved choice outranks the default in both directions.
#
# scx.service itself is never `enable`d: novadeck-powerd starts it when the selected scheduler
# says to (DEFAULT_CPU_SCHEDULER is the fallback when nothing is saved) and stops it on "none".
# Do not add a preset or a .wants symlink for it — powerd must own the PowerProfiles bus names
# before lavd's --autopower reads them, and enabling the unit breaks that ordering.
#
# The skeleton-generation failure is SOLVED — root-caused, not worked around blind. Every BPF
# build script used to die with:
#
#   thread 'main' panicked at scheds/rust/scx_bpfland/build.rs:12:10:
#     failed to generate .../bpf.bpf.o
#     0: Failed to generate skeleton for .../bpf.bpf.o
#     1: Broken pipe (os error 32)
#
# bpftool was NOT the cause — an earlier note here blamed it, wrongly. libbpf-cargo 0.26
# generates skeletons in-process and never spawns bpftool. The real cause is scx_cargo's
# "skip formatting" sentinel colliding with a qemu bug; the fix is the `disable_rustfmt` shim
# in the PKGBUILD's build(), which is commented in full there. Short version: scx_cargo calls
# .rustfmt("disable_rustfmt") expecting the spawn to fail with NotFound so libbpf-cargo leaves
# the generated code untouched, but under x86 user-mode qemu Command::spawn() returns Ok for a
# PATH name that does not exist, the child exits 127, and the write to its stdin gets EPIPE.
# Measured in the pinned base-devel image: spawn("disable_rustfmt") -> Ok + exit 127 +
# BrokenPipe, spawn("cat") -> Ok and round-trips stdin. So build() puts an identity-filter
# `disable_rustfmt` on PATH, which is exactly the no-op upstream intends.
#
# The general trap, worth remembering beyond this package: under emulation a Rust
# Command::spawn() of a missing binary does not report NotFound. Any upstream code that treats
# "spawn fails" as a feature-detection signal will take the wrong branch here.
#
# What is already ruled IN (do not re-litigate):
#   - options=(!lto) is REQUIRED and CONFIRMED. Without it the build dies earlier, linking
#     scx_arena's build script with `undefined symbol: bpf_linker__new` / `bpf_object__open_mem`,
#     because holo's base-devel makepkg.conf turns LTO on globally and LTO over libbpf-sys's
#     static archive drops those symbols. Arch's own scx-scheds carries the same option.
#   - Every depends/makedepends name here is verified to resolve via `pacman -Si` in the pinned
#     base-devel image. Two names copied from other distros' recipes did NOT: `libelf.so` (use
#     elfutils) and `libgcc` (use gcc-libs). Verify names before a build, never during one.
#   - The clang invocation itself is fine: clang 21.1.5 runs with -D__TARGET_ARCH_arm64, -mcpu=v3
#     and the vendored arm64 vmlinux.h, and preprocesses the system headers successfully.
#
# See also the [[scx-sched-bringup]] memory note.
#
# scx-scheds — built FROM SOURCE via a novadeck-owned PKGBUILD (packages/scx-scheds/PKGBUILD),
# NOT fetched from the holo monorepo: holo has no scx-scheds to fetch a PKGBUILD for (SteamOS
# ships none). This is the "local PKGBUILD" form, like packages/mesa and packages/gtk2; makepkg
# still downloads the upstream release tarball named in the PKGBUILD.
#
# Why: sched_ext lets the CPU scheduler be implemented as a BPF program loaded from userspace.
# scx_lavd is the gaming-oriented one (latency-criticality aware, and it traces futex calls to
# avoid lock-holder preemption). The kernel half — CONFIG_SCHED_CLASS_EXT plus the BTF chain it
# depends on — is in kernel/kernel.config; without it the schedulers here cannot attach, since
# libbpf resolves struct sched_ext_ops out of /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux at load time.
#
# Fields:
#   name           pkgname (must match the PKGBUILD); also the work/ build-dir + repo entry
#   pkgbuild_local path (relative to this dir) to the checked-in PKGBUILD to build
#   pkgrel_suffix  numeric suffix appended to the PKGBUILD's pkgrel (N -> N.<suffix>) so pacman
#                  prefers our build on later upgrades. Integer only.
#
# ONE patch, and it is a straight cherry-pick of upstream 6d31ddd8 (2026-08-21), which is fixed
# on upstream main but has NOT been released — v1.1.3 is still the newest tag. Drop this patch at
# the first release that contains the commit; the build fails loudly if it no longer applies.
#
# It fixes the RCU/task stall this device hit within ~30s of every gameplay attach at 1.1.3
# (2026-08-27, Darksiders): lavd's REENQ path reused a cached suggested_cpu_id without checking
# it against p->cpus_ptr, so a task whose cpus_ptr had narrowed underneath it (migrate_disable,
# sched_setaffinity, cgroup migration, hotplug) was routed to the per-CPU DSQ of a CPU that
# cannot run it, and starved there. Our journal signature matched upstream's report almost
# exactly — upstream reported runnable_at=-21185ms, we saw -21000/-21004/-21008/-21024ms across
# five consecutive ejections, each followed by `RCU CPU stall detected!`.
#
# Why 1.1.3 and not 1.1.2, which ran this same code path: 1.1.3 moved the re-enqueue TRIGGER off
# ops.cpu_release onto the `?tp_btf/sched_switch` hook, which fires on every fair -> RT/DL
# transition system-wide instead of only when a higher class preempts an scx CPU. That makes the
# REENQ path enormously hotter, which is what turns a latent staleness bug into a stall every
# half-minute. Our lavd_ops.flags also carries SCX_OPS_ENQ_MIGRATION_DISABLED, which routes
# exactly the migrate_disable tasks in upstream's report through ops.enqueue. That mechanism is
# OUR inference, not upstream's claim — upstream only documents the staleness itself.
#
# NOT fixed here, deliberately: scheds/rust/scx_lavd/src/bpf/main.bpf.c still writes
# `p->scx.dsq_vtime` directly at one site in the same REENQ branch, where all six sibling call
# sites were migrated to scx_bpf_task_set_slice/_dsq_vtime. That is the sole source of the
# kernel's `Writing directly to p->scx.slice/dsq_vtime is deprecated` warning, which therefore
# did NOT go away on this bump the way the 1.1.2 note predicted it would. It is cosmetic — it is
# not the stall, and it is still unfixed on upstream main (no compat.bpf.h commits since the
# tag) — so it stays unpatched rather than carrying a speculative local delta. Report upstream.
#
# To bump scx: edit pkgver + sha256sums in packages/scx-scheds/PKGBUILD, and re-check that
# services/scx.service and services/scx still exist at those paths in the new tarball (package()
# installs both). Also re-read upstream's own kernel.config in the tarball root — it is the
# authoritative list of required CONFIG_* symbols, and it does change between releases.
#
# Bump to 1.1.3 (upstream release 2026-08-19), checked 2026-08-27 against the v1.1.3 tarball:
#   - services/scx.service and services/scx both still present, and services/scx still has the
#     three lines package()'s sed rewrites (`SCX_SCHEDULER=`, the `# Set custom flags` comment,
#     `#SCX_FLAGS=`). The `SCX_FLAGS='--autopower'` grep assertion therefore still fires.
#   - scheds/rust/scx_{lavd,bpfland,rusty} all still exist, Cargo.lock is still checked in
#     (--locked/--frozen hold), and scx_cargo still uses the `disable_rustfmt` sentinel at three
#     call sites — so the qemu shim in build() still matches and is still needed.
#   - upstream kernel.config is BYTE-IDENTICAL to 1.1.2's. Nothing to do in kernel/kernel.config.
#
# One new runtime dependency that upstream's kernel.config already covers but our fragment does
# not name, so it was traced rather than assumed. 1.1.3's scx_lavd drops ops.cpu_acquire/release
# on kernels >= 6.19 and attaches `SEC("?tp_btf/sched_switch")` instead — ours is 7.2, so that
# path IS taken and CONFIG_BPF_EVENTS is now load-bearing where it was not at 1.1.2. It resolves
# to =y here without an entry: on arm64 ARCH_SUPPORTS_UPROBES is def_bool y, so UPROBE_EVENTS
# takes its `default y`, and BPF_EVENTS is a promptless `default y` gated on
# (KPROBE_EVENTS || UPROBE_EVENTS) && PERF_EVENTS && BPF_SYSCALL, all of which hold. Do NOT add
# CONFIG_BPF_EVENTS to kernel/kernel.config — it has no prompt and is already right.
# The other kfuncs 1.1.3 migrated to (scx_bpf_task_set_slice/_dsq_vtime, added in 6.19) are
# __weak ksyms behind bpf_ksym_exists() in scheds/include/scx/compat.bpf.h, so they are safe on
# an older kernel too.

name: scx-scheds
pkgbuild_local: PKGBUILD
patches: 0001-scx_lavd-validate-cached-cpu-pick-on-reenq.patch
pkgrel_suffix: 2
